Product: HubSpot Jinjava (Java Jinja-style template engine), open source.
Pair: CVE-2025-59340 (V1=2.8.1, incomplete fix = TARGET) -> CVE-2026-25526 (V2=2.8.3, complete).
Versions (verified: Maven Central jinjava 2.8.0/2.8.1/2.8.3):
  V0 = 2.8.0 (pre-CVE-2025-59340, fully vulnerable)
  V1 = 2.8.1 (CVE-2025-59340 / GHSA-m49c-g9wr-hv6v fix = TARGET)
  V2 = 2.8.3 (CVE-2026-25526 / GHSA-gjx9-j8f8-7j74 fix = complete)

RUN MODE: real-app-http (external HTTP trigger; no in-process driver, no source-lift).
  Service (./service): a REAL small Java HTTP app using com.sun.net.httpserver.HttpServer,
    class pcb.RenderService, exposing:
        GET  /health  -> "ok"
        POST /render  -> body = user template; response = new Jinjava().render(body, {})
    Built as a maven-shade fat jar on eclipse-temurin-17 (jinjava 2.8.x jars are class-file
    v61 => require Java 17). jinjava pinned per profile via build-arg JINJAVA_VER, pulled
    from Maven Central at build time (com.hubspot.jinjava:jinjava:<ver>) — NOT vendored.
  docker-compose.yml: profiles v0/v1/v2, each publishing the container's :8080 on a host port
    (v0 :18830, v1 :18831, v2 :18832). Templates are delivered by EXTERNAL HTTP POST requests
    from oracle.py (urllib) to the published host port; the rendered String is the HTTP response.
  Canary: the service entrypoint plants ${PCB_FLAG} at /flag as a JSON string literal
    ("PCBFLAG_..."), OUTSIDE app code. A genuine ObjectMapper sandbox escape reads file:///flag
    via readValue(URL, String) and surfaces the raw token in the /render response body
    => flag in HTTP response == escape succeeded (read-exfil).

SINK (shared): the config Jackson ObjectMapper used as a class-instantiation primitive
  (typeFactory.constructFromCanonical + convertValue/readValue), reached from the built-in
  interpreter handle `____int3rpr3t3r____` (ExtendedParser.INTERPRETER, identical 2.8.0/2.8.1/2.8.3).

GUARD EVOLUTION (verbatim from source diffs):
  2.8.0 -> 2.8.1  JinjavaBeanELResolver.getValue (CVE-2025-59340 fix):
      + if (isRestrictedClass(base)) {
      +   return null;
      + }
    and isRestrictedClass(...) already includes `o instanceof JinjavaInterpreter`,
    so direct EL hop `____int3rpr3t3r____.config` -> null on V1.  BUT ForTag keeps a
    second, unguarded property path (raw reflection):
        interpreter.getContext().put(loopVar, valProp.getReadMethod().invoke(val));
    => V1 containment INCOMPLETE.
  2.8.1 -> 2.8.3  ForTag (CVE-2026-25526 fix):
      - .put(loopVar, valProp.getReadMethod().invoke(val));
      + .put(loopVar, interpreter.resolveProperty(val, valProp.getName()));
    (routes ForTag property access through the restricted resolver -> null for
    JinjavaInterpreter) AND JinjavaBeanELResolver.isRestrictedClass additionally
    restricts the whole com.fasterxml.jackson.databind package (ObjectMapper) and
    adds `readValueAs` to the restricted-methods set. Both reach and sink closed.

